# What does NOT go into an installation.
#
# `composer install` from Packagist fetches a DIST ARCHIVE built from the git
# tree, and everything tracked here is in it unless said otherwise. Without this
# file every consumer downloaded the package's own 548K test suite, its Testbench
# host and its PHPUnit config - none of which they can run, because the tools
# those need are `require-dev` and dev requirements of a DEPENDENCY are never
# installed.
#
# So it was not merely wasted bytes; it was a `tests/` directory inside
# `vendor/` that looks runnable and is not, and a `phpunit.xml` sitting where a
# test runner scanning the tree might pick it up.
#
# `export-ignore` is the mechanism Laravel, Symfony and Filament all use for
# exactly this. The source of truth stays the repository - nothing here deletes
# anything - it only bounds what an installation receives.

/tests            export-ignore
/phpunit.xml      export-ignore
/testbench.yaml   export-ignore
/.gitattributes   export-ignore

# THE LOCKFILE IS DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED. A library's `composer.lock` is never
# read by a consumer - only the root project's is - and shipping one invites the
# reader to believe those are the versions they will get.
/composer.lock    export-ignore

# `resources/client` STAYS. It is the mirrored copy of the npm package that the
# installer publishes into an application, so it is not a build artefact here -
# it is the thing being distributed. See `make sync-client`.
